# Trivy CVE allowlist - OPS-3 / build-and-push security gate.
#
# Each entry MUST include the CVE ID, the package, the rationale, and an
# expiry date. CI re-evaluates on every build; an unjustified entry will be
# spotted in code review.
#
# REQUIRED machine-readable fields (parsed by api/src/scripts/check-trivyignore.ts):
#   Class:       judgment | quarantine-window
#   Reviewed:    <YYYY-MM-DD>   when the rationale below was last validated
#   Re-check-by: <YYYY-MM-DD>   deadline for the next periodic review
#
# ENTRY CLASSES - pick the right one, the cadences differ by 13x
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Class: judgment
#   A standing decision that the CVE is not worth acting on - e.g. upstream
#   published a "fix" and then deprecated it, or the vulnerable sink is not
#   reachable in our usage. Open-ended. Default cadence: 90 days.
#   This is the DEFAULT when no `Class:` field is present, so pre-existing
#   entries keep working.
#
# Class: quarantine-window
#   A fix EXISTS and we intend to take it, but the fixed version is younger
#   than the 7-day corporate minimum release age, so installing it today would
#   violate the supply-chain policy. This is purely a TIMING wait.
#   Additionally REQUIRED:
#     Fixed-version:      <the version that resolves the CVE>
#     Fix-available-from: <YYYY-MM-DD - the date it clears the 7-day window>
#   Capped at 14 days (MAX_QUARANTINE_WINDOW_DAYS). The checker also flags the
#   entry as actionable the moment `Fix-available-from` passes, independent of
#   `Re-check-by`, so a known-fixed HIGH CVE is never left suppressed.
#   NEVER extend one of these to buy time. If it must outlive the cap it is a
#   `judgment` entry and needs a written rationale.
#
# Why the distinction exists: a 7-day timing hold and an indefinite judgment
# call look identical in this file, so the 90-day default silently swallowed
# the former. See docs/strategy/NPM_SUPPLY_CHAIN_QUARANTINE_POLICY.md.
#
# Trivy itself only reads bare CVE IDs; the surrounding comments are for
# humans and the review-staleness check (.github/workflows/trivyignore-review.yml).

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CVE-2026-4800 - lodash 4.17.21 → "fixed" in 4.18.0
# Package: lodash (transitive via @chevrotain/cst-dts-gen, @chevrotain/gast,
#                  pulled in by Prisma 7's @prisma/internals)
# Rationale: lodash 4.18.0 was published, then deprecated by the maintainers
#            with the explicit notice "Bad release. Please use lodash@4.17.21
#            instead." (visible via `npm view lodash@4.18.0`). Forcing 4.18.0
#            via npm overrides reintroduces a regression worse than the CVE
#            (which requires attacker-controlled input to a lodash sink we do
#            not expose - chevrotain only uses lodash internally for AST work
#            on Prisma schema files at build time, never at request time).
# Owner:       @pranems
# Class:       judgment
# Reviewed:    2026-04-30
# Re-check-by: 2026-07-30
# Re-check action: when chevrotain or lodash publishes a real fix, drop the
#                  override and remove this entry.
CVE-2026-4800

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CVE-2026-18446 (GHSA-7p8r-x3mc-p8w7) - fast-uri 3.1.4, HIGH
# Package: fast-uri, pinned by an explicit override in api/package.json.
#          Reached via prisma -> @prisma/dev -> @prisma/streams-local -> ajv.
# Advisory published 2026-08-03T19:16Z. "fast-uri vulnerable to host confusion
#          via backslash authority introducer."
#
# Why suppressed, and only until 2026-08-10:
#   Fixed versions 2.4.4 / 3.1.5 / 4.1.2 were all published within the last
#   7 days, so the corporate feed proxy does not serve them - measured:
#   `npm view fast-uri versions` tops out at 4.1.1. Taking the fix today is
#   impossible without violating the NPM Minimum Release control, and CI holds
#   itself to the same bar (see .github/workflows/regen-lockfile.yml).
#   This is a TIMING wait, not a judgement that the CVE is acceptable.
#
# Reachability while we wait: fast-uri parses JSON-Schema $ref URIs inside ajv,
#   which this repo reaches only through Prisma's CLI/dev tooling. It is not on
#   any request path, so the host-confusion vector is not exposed to callers.
#
# Owner:              @pranems
# Class:              quarantine-window
# Fixed-version:      4.1.2
# Fix-available-from: 2026-08-10
# Reviewed:           2026-08-04
# Re-check-by:        2026-08-10
# Re-check action: bump the `fast-uri` override in api/package.json to 4.1.2,
#                  regenerate the lockfile via the regen-lockfile workflow (NOT
#                  locally - a corp device produces a contaminated lockfile),
#                  and DELETE this entry in the same commit.
CVE-2026-18446
